⚡ How to Simulate Link Failure and Verify OSPF Fault Tolerance

1. Verify Neighbor Relationships:
Run show ip ospf neighbor on any router. You should see adjacent routers listed in a FULL state.
2. Check the Routing Table:
Run show ip route. OSPF routes will be populated dynamically and marked with an O.
3. Trace Route:
Run a traceroute 192.168.3.10 from a PC on Router 1 to a PC on Router 3. Note down the path it takes (e.g., passing through Router 2).
4. Induce Link Failure:
Go to Router 1 and shut down the interface leading to Router 2:text
Router1(config)# interface Serial0/0/0
Router1(config-if)# shutdown
Use code with caution.
5. Observe Convergence:
OSPF will automatically detect the drop after the dead interval, flood link-state updates (LSAs), and recalculate the Shortest Path First (SPF) tree.
6. Verify Redirection:
Run traceroute 192.168.3.10 again. The traffic will automatically bypass the broken link and route dynamically through Router 4 to reach Router 3 without manual intervention.
